# Request membership

Most access to a Nocturne instance starts with an invite: the owner sends a link and the person
accepts it. **Membership requests** turn that around. Someone who lands on the instance can ask
the owner for access, and the owner decides. Asking grants nothing on its own.

It is **on by default**, and each instance owner can switch it off.

## What the visitor sees

At the foot of the sign-in card there's a **Request membership** link, under the passkey and
social-login buttons; see the annotated sign-in screen on
[Authentication](/docs/authentication#what-your-users-see).

Following it opens a short dialog. Type your name and a message that says who you are and
why you ask, then press **Send request**. Your device then asks you to make a passkey. The
passkey is how you sign in after the owner approves the request:

<Screenshot id="request-membership-dialog" />

A useful note is short and says who you are in terms the owner will recognise: the name
they know you by, and your connection to them. The owner sees the note next to your request
when they decide, so a blank one is simply harder to say yes to.

When the request is sent, the dialog says so. You cannot sign in until the owner approves the
request. If your browser cannot make a passkey, the dialog tells you before you type anything;
use a browser or device that supports passkeys.

<Callout type="info" title="Nothing happens until the owner approves">

  Sending a request does not create access. Until the owner approves it and picks a role,
  you see nothing of their data.

</Callout>

## What the owner sees

The owner can switch requests on and off on **Site settings > Sharing & Privacy**. A request
sent from the sign-in page appears on that same page, under **Membership requests**, and the
owner gets a notification. They see the name, the note, and when it was sent, and approve it
with a role or deny it. When they approve it, your account becomes active and your passkey
signs you in. When they deny it, your account stays inactive.

## Access end date

When the owner approves a request, they can set an end date in the **Access ends (optional)**
field. Access then ends on a day that the owner chooses. If the owner leaves the field empty, the
access has no end date.

The rules for an end date:

- **Access holds through the end of that day.** The end date is the last day of access. The day
  follows the time zone of the device that the approver uses.
- **Nothing is revoked.** After the end date, your account stops giving access, but it keeps the
  same role. If the owner sets a later date, you have the same access again.
- **An owner has no end date.** An owner's access does not end.
- **A person whose own access ends cannot give access that lasts longer.** If an approver's access
  ends on a date, the access that they approve ends on that day or on an earlier day.

For how to set, change, or remove an end date, see
[Member accounts and invites](/docs/sharing/members#access-end-date).

<Callout type="tip" title="Approve deliberately">

  A request tells you what someone says about themselves, and nothing more. If a request is
  unexpected, or the note doesn't match anyone you know, deny it; someone who genuinely
  needs access can ask again, or you can send them an invite directly.

</Callout>
